{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgcVATxDqKUNeLbW9FcDB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011swAbQGux7NyMK4LYoggXB","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/repo/docs/deploy.md","content":"# Deploy\n\nRedmine is a product that organizations install on their own servers. The repository has no Dockerfile, Procfile, Capistrano file, Helm chart or deploy workflow. The generic procedure is in `doc/INSTALL` (new installation) and `doc/UPGRADING` (upgrade). This page summarizes it and lists what the repository does not say about this copy.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a human.** Is this copy of Redmine deployed anywhere by your team (which server, which environments), or is it only a mirror of upstream used for development?\n> Why it matters and how to do it: https://docs.unwritten.sh/harness/guides/deploy/\n<!-- uw-harness:todo deploy-explicit -->\n\n## Environments\n\nThe code knows three Rails environments: `development`, `test` and `production` (`config/environments/`). Production settings that the code reads are in `config/database.yml`, `config/configuration.yml` and `config/additional_environment.rb`, all ignored by git and created from their `.example` files. No staging or production host is named in the repository.\n\n## How a release is built\n\nUpstream releases are source archives and tags (see `doc/CHANGELOG`). There is no build artifact: the server runs the source tree. On the server, the steps from `doc/INSTALL` are:\n\n1. Write `config/database.yml` for the production database.\n2. `bundle install --without development test` (installs the adapter gem named in `config/database.yml`).\n3. `bin/rails generate_secret_token` (writes `config/initializers/secret_token.rb`).\n4. Optional: `bin/rails redmine:mermaid:install RAILS_ENV=\"production\"` (downloads Mermaid.js from registry.npmjs.org).\n5. Optional: `bin/rails assets:precompile RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`.\n\n## How it is deployed\n\n1. `bin/rails db:migrate RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`, and `bin/rails redmine:plugins:migrate RAILS_ENV=\"production\"` when plugins are installed.\n2. On a new installation only: `bin/rails redmine:load_default_data RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`.\n3. Give the application user write access to `files/`, `log/`, `tmp/` and `public/assets` (see `doc/INSTALL`, step 9).\n4. After an upgrade: `bin/rails tmp:cache:clear tmp:sessions:clear`, then restart the application server.\n5. Start the server, for example `bin/rails server -e production` (Puma), or behind Passenger or another Rack server.\n\nFor an upgrade, `doc/UPGRADING` adds: copy `config/database.yml`, `config/configuration.yml`, the `files/` folder and the `plugins/` and `themes/` folders from the old installation.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a human.** If this copy is deployed: who can deploy, how is a release built for it (git tag, archive, branch), and how do you roll back a release that ran database migrations?\n> Why it matters and how to do it: https://docs.unwritten.sh/harness/guides/deploy/\n<!-- uw-harness:todo deploy-explicit -->\n\n## How to roll back\n\nThe repository shows only the generic Rails mechanism: `bin/rails db:rollback RAILS_ENV=\"production\" STEP=n` reverts migrations, then the previous source tree is put back. Not every migration is reversible, so a database backup before `db:migrate` is the safe path. The rollback procedure for this copy is one of the questions above.\n\n## What to check after a deploy\n\n- `GET /up` answers 200 (Rails health check, `config/routes.rb`).\n- Admin > Information (`/admin/info`) shows all migrations run, `files/` writable, the default admin password changed, and, in production, a warning when the ActiveJob queue adapter is still `:async`.\n- `log/production.log` (or STDOUT when `RAILS_LOG_TO_STDOUT` is set) has no errors. See [observability.md](observability.md).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":297,"cache_read_input_tokens":120337,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":297},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98c9b43a-1aa1-4043-94cd-7a9dd1b3395e","uuid":"55edbafc-90b5-4d00-9b81-213df73e56e5","timestamp":"2026-11-01T16:00:12.851Z","request_id":"req_011CgcVAThbMKCvZjF7UNzA7","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011swAbQGux7NyMK4LYoggXB":{"file_path":"/Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/repo/docs/deploy.md","content":"# Deploy\n\nRedmine is a product that organizations install on their own servers. The repository has no Dockerfile, Procfile, Capistrano file, Helm chart or deploy workflow. The generic procedure is in `doc/INSTALL` (new installation) and `doc/UPGRADING` (upgrade). This page summarizes it and lists what the repository does not say about this copy.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a human.** Is this copy of Redmine deployed anywhere by your team (which server, which environments), or is it only a mirror of upstream used for development?\n> Why it matters and how to do it: https://docs.unwritten.sh/harness/guides/deploy/\n<!-- uw-harness:todo deploy-explicit -->\n\n## Environments\n\nThe code knows three Rails environments: `development`, `test` and `production` (`config/environments/`). Production settings that the code reads are in `config/database.yml`, `config/configuration.yml` and `config/additional_environment.rb`, all ignored by git and created from their `.example` files. No staging or production host is named in the repository.\n\n## How a release is built\n\nUpstream releases are source archives and tags (see `doc/CHANGELOG`). There is no build artifact: the server runs the source tree. On the server, the steps from `doc/INSTALL` are:\n\n1. Write `config/database.yml` for the production database.\n2. `bundle install --without development test` (installs the adapter gem named in `config/database.yml`).\n3. `bin/rails generate_secret_token` (writes `config/initializers/secret_token.rb`).\n4. Optional: `bin/rails redmine:mermaid:install RAILS_ENV=\"production\"` (downloads Mermaid.js from registry.npmjs.org).\n5. Optional: `bin/rails assets:precompile RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`.\n\n## How it is deployed\n\n1. `bin/rails db:migrate RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`, and `bin/rails redmine:plugins:migrate RAILS_ENV=\"production\"` when plugins are installed.\n2. On a new installation only: `bin/rails redmine:load_default_data RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`.\n3. Give the application user write access to `files/`, `log/`, `tmp/` and `public/assets` (see `doc/INSTALL`, step 9).\n4. After an upgrade: `bin/rails tmp:cache:clear tmp:sessions:clear`, then restart the application server.\n5. Start the server, for example `bin/rails server -e production` (Puma), or behind Passenger or another Rack server.\n\nFor an upgrade, `doc/UPGRADING` adds: copy `config/database.yml`, `config/configuration.yml`, the `files/` folder and the `plugins/` and `themes/` folders from the old installation.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a human.** If this copy is deployed: who can deploy, how is a release built for it (git tag, archive, branch), and how do you roll back a release that ran database migrations?\n> Why it matters and how to do it: https://docs.unwritten.sh/harness/guides/deploy/\n<!-- uw-harness:todo deploy-explicit -->\n\n## How to roll back\n\nThe repository shows only the generic Rails mechanism: `bin/rails db:rollback RAILS_ENV=\"production\" STEP=n` reverts migrations, then the previous source tree is put back. Not every migration is reversible, so a database backup before `db:migrate` is the safe path. The rollback procedure for this copy is one of the questions above.\n\n## What to check after a deploy\n\n- `GET /up` answers 200 (Rails health check, `config/routes.rb`).\n- Admin > Information (`/admin/info`) shows all migrations run, `files/` writable, the default admin password changed, and, in production, a warning when the ActiveJob queue adapter is still `:async`.\n- `log/production.log` (or STDOUT when `RAILS_LOG_TO_STDOUT` is set) has no errors. See [observability.md](observability.md).\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011swAbQGux7NyMK4LYoggXB","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/repo/docs/deploy.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98c9b43a-1aa1-4043-94cd-7a9dd1b3395e","uuid":"4a245ef1-5bdd-48e9-be73-31b820666d29","timestamp":"2026-11-01T16:00:12.877Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/repo/docs/deploy.md","content":"# Deploy\n\nRedmine is a product that organizations install on their own servers. The repository has no Dockerfile, Procfile, Capistrano file, Helm chart or deploy workflow. The generic procedure is in `doc/INSTALL` (new installation) and `doc/UPGRADING` (upgrade). This page summarizes it and lists what the repository does not say about this copy.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a human.** Is this copy of Redmine deployed anywhere by your team (which server, which environments), or is it only a mirror of upstream used for development?\n> Why it matters and how to do it: https://docs.unwritten.sh/harness/guides/deploy/\n<!-- uw-harness:todo deploy-explicit -->\n\n## Environments\n\nThe code knows three Rails environments: `development`, `test` and `production` (`config/environments/`). Production settings that the code reads are in `config/database.yml`, `config/configuration.yml` and `config/additional_environment.rb`, all ignored by git and created from their `.example` files. No staging or production host is named in the repository.\n\n## How a release is built\n\nUpstream releases are source archives and tags (see `doc/CHANGELOG`). There is no build artifact: the server runs the source tree. On the server, the steps from `doc/INSTALL` are:\n\n1. Write `config/database.yml` for the production database.\n2. `bundle install --without development test` (installs the adapter gem named in `config/database.yml`).\n3. `bin/rails generate_secret_token` (writes `config/initializers/secret_token.rb`).\n4. Optional: `bin/rails redmine:mermaid:install RAILS_ENV=\"production\"` (downloads Mermaid.js from registry.npmjs.org).\n5. Optional: `bin/rails assets:precompile RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`.\n\n## How it is deployed\n\n1. `bin/rails db:migrate RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`, and `bin/rails redmine:plugins:migrate RAILS_ENV=\"production\"` when plugins are installed.\n2. On a new installation only: `bin/rails redmine:load_default_data RAILS_ENV=\"production\"`.\n3. Give the application user write access to `files/`, `log/`, `tmp/` and `public/assets` (see `doc/INSTALL`, step 9).\n4. After an upgrade: `bin/rails tmp:cache:clear tmp:sessions:clear`, then restart the application server.\n5. Start the server, for example `bin/rails server -e production` (Puma), or behind Passenger or another Rack server.\n\nFor an upgrade, `doc/UPGRADING` adds: copy `config/database.yml`, `config/configuration.yml`, the `files/` folder and the `plugins/` and `themes/` folders from the old installation.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a human.** If this copy is deployed: who can deploy, how is a release built for it (git tag, archive, branch), and how do you roll back a release that ran database migrations?\n> Why it matters and how to do it: https://docs.unwritten.sh/harness/guides/deploy/\n<!-- uw-harness:todo deploy-explicit -->\n\n## How to roll back\n\nThe repository shows only the generic Rails mechanism: `bin/rails db:rollback RAILS_ENV=\"production\" STEP=n` reverts migrations, then the previous source tree is put back. Not every migration is reversible, so a database backup before `db:migrate` is the safe path. The rollback procedure for this copy is one of the questions above.\n\n## What to check after a deploy\n\n- `GET /up` answers 200 (Rails health check, `config/routes.rb`).\n- Admin > Information (`/admin/info`) shows all migrations run, `files/` writable, the default admin password changed, and, in production, a warning when the ActiveJob queue adapter is still `:async`.\n- `log/production.log` (or STDOUT when `RAILS_LOG_TO_STDOUT` is set) has no errors. See [observability.md](observability.md).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011swAbQGux7NyMK4LYoggXB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgcVATxDqKUNeLbW9FcDB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X5Q8Pwq6dYxUSy5sPoZhMt","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/repo/docs/observability.md","content":"# Observability\n\nThis page lists what the code provides to watch a running Redmine. The repository has no error tracker (Sentry or similar), no metrics library and no tracing in the `Gemfile`. Where the logs of a production instance go is not visible here.\n\n## Logs\n\n- Library: the Rails logger (`ActiveSupport::Logger`).\n- Level: `info` in production, `debug` elsewhere (`config/application.rb`).\n- Destination: `log/<environment>.log` by default. In production, setting `RAILS_LOG_TO_STDOUT` sends logs to STDOUT with `ActiveSupport::TaggedLogging` (`config/environments/production.rb`). `config/additional_environment.rb` can set another logger, for example syslog (commented example in `production.rb`).\n- Mail delivery is not logged in production (`config.action_mailer.logger = nil`).\n- `ApplicationController` logs invalid authenticity tokens and invalid query statements at `error` level (`app/controllers/application_controller.rb`).\n\n## Health and status\n\n- `GET /up`: Rails health check (`rails/health#show`, `config/routes.rb`). It returns 200 when the app boots without exceptions and 500 otherwise. It does not check the database.\n- Admin > Information (`/admin/info`, `AdminController#info`): checklist with default admin password changed, `files/` writable, pending migrations, ImageMagick, Ghostscript, Pandoc and Mermaid availability, and in production a warning when the ActiveJob queue adapter is `:async`.\n- Background jobs: `WebhookJob` delivers webhooks. Without a queue adapter configured, jobs run in threads of the web process and are lost on restart.\n\n## How to tell that something is wrong\n\n1. `GET /up` does not answer 200.\n2. `log/production.log` (or the STDOUT stream) shows `FATAL` or `ERROR` lines, or 500 responses (`Completed 500`).\n3. Admin > Information shows a red item, for example pending migrations after an upgrade.\n4. Users report that email notifications, incoming email or repository updates stopped: those depend on SMTP, on the cron jobs for `redmine:email:receive_imap` or `redmine:fetch_changesets`, and on the SCM binaries, which run outside the web process.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a human.** Where do production logs go (log/production.log, STDOUT to a collector?), and is there any error tracker, dashboard or alert channel for this instance?\n> Why it matters and how to do it: https://docs.unwritten.sh/harness/guides/observability/\n<!-- uw-harness:todo observability -->\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":297,"cache_read_input_tokens":120337,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":297},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98c9b43a-1aa1-4043-94cd-7a9dd1b3395e","uuid":"82ada567-a6c6-42f9-9a41-3e0dd844e951","timestamp":"2026-11-01T16:00:21.005Z","request_id":"req_011CgcVAThbMKCvZjF7UNzA7","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01X5Q8Pwq6dYxUSy5sPoZhMt":{"file_path":"/Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/repo/docs/observability.md","content":"# Observability\n\nThis page lists what the code provides to watch a running Redmine. The repository has no error tracker (Sentry or similar), no metrics library and no tracing in the `Gemfile`. Where the logs of a production instance go is not visible here.\n\n## Logs\n\n- Library: the Rails logger (`ActiveSupport::Logger`).\n- Level: `info` in production, `debug` elsewhere (`config/application.rb`).\n- Destination: `log/<environment>.log` by default. In production, setting `RAILS_LOG_TO_STDOUT` sends logs to STDOUT with `ActiveSupport::TaggedLogging` (`config/environments/production.rb`). `config/additional_environment.rb` can set another logger, for example syslog (commented example in `production.rb`).\n- Mail delivery is not logged in production (`config.action_mailer.logger = nil`).\n- `ApplicationController` logs invalid authenticity tokens and invalid query statements at `error` level (`app/controllers/application_controller.rb`).\n\n## Health and status\n\n- `GET /up`: Rails health check (`rails/health#show`, `config/routes.rb`). It returns 200 when the app boots without exceptions and 500 otherwise. It does not check the database.\n- Admin > Information (`/admin/info`, `AdminController#info`): checklist with default admin password changed, `files/` writable, pending migrations, ImageMagick, Ghostscript, Pandoc and Mermaid availability, and in production a warning when the ActiveJob queue adapter is `:async`.\n- Background jobs: `WebhookJob` delivers webhooks. Without a queue adapter configured, jobs run in threads of the web process and are lost on restart.\n\n## How to tell that something is wrong\n\n1. `GET /up` does not answer 200.\n2. `log/production.log` (or the STDOUT stream) shows `FATAL` or `ERROR` lines, or 500 responses (`Completed 500`).\n3. Admin > Information shows a red item, for example pending migrations after an upgrade.\n4. Users report that email notifications, incoming email or repository updates stopped: those depend on SMTP, on the cron jobs for `redmine:email:receive_imap` or `redmine:fetch_changesets`, and on the SCM binaries, which run outside the web process.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a human.** Where do production logs go (log/production.log, STDOUT to a collector?), and is there any error tracker, dashboard or alert channel for this instance?\n> Why it matters and how to do it: https://docs.unwritten.sh/harness/guides/observability/\n<!-- uw-harness:todo observability -->\n"}}}
