{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgcVCuZQMW1P6hro1YhJm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TRpv1oqzLY6z9DRaH4RnsW","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/repo; mkdir -p .agents/skills/{implement-change,debug-local,write-migration,frontend-stimulus}\ncat > .agents/skills/implement-change/SKILL.md <<'EOF'\n---\nname: implement-change\ndescription: Use when you take a task (bug fix, feature, refactor) in Redmine and need to go from the request to a verified change.\n---\n\n# Implement a change\n\n1. Make sure the environment is ready: `bin/harness setup` (safe to run again). It writes `config/database.yml` for SQLite3 if missing, installs gems and prepares the databases.\n2. Find the code. Start from the route in `config/routes.rb`, then the controller in `app/controllers`, the model in `app/models` and the library code in `lib/redmine`. `docs/product.md` maps each use case to its entry points; `docs/architecture.md` explains the components.\n3. Find the existing tests for that code: `test/unit/<model>_test.rb`, `test/functional/<controller>_test.rb`, `test/integration/` (API tests in `test/integration/api_test/`), `test/system/` (browser). Test data comes from `test/fixtures/*.yml`.\n4. Write or update a test first and run it alone: `bin/rails test test/unit/issue_test.rb -n test_create` (use `-n /pattern/` for several).\n5. Make the change. Keep the existing style: RuboCop config in `.rubocop.yml`, hash rockets are common in old code, user-visible strings go in `config/locales/en.yml`.\n6. If the change needs a schema change, follow the `write-migration` skill. If it touches JavaScript or CSS, follow the `frontend-stimulus` skill.\n7. Run the fast gate: `bin/harness check-fast` (RuboCop, Stylelint, unit and helper tests, about 40 s). Fix every offense and failure. `bundle exec rubocop -a <file>` fixes most style offenses.\n8. When the change touches controllers, views, routes or the API, run the full gate: `bin/harness check` (all tests except system tests, about 3 minutes). For UI behavior, also run the system test you changed: `bin/rails test test/system/issues_test.rb` (needs Google Chrome).\n9. Write the commit message in the upstream style: one imperative sentence ending with the redmine.org issue number, for example `Fix missing Gantt expanders for projects with only subprojects (#44694).` See `docs/agreements/working-agreements.md`.\n10. Add a dated entry to `docs/harness/log.md` for a significant change: what changed, what you learned, what is left.\nEOF\ncat > .agents/skills/debug-local/SKILL.md <<'EOF'\n---\nname: debug-local\ndescription: Use when a Redmine test fails, a page errors, or behavior is wrong on your machine and you need to reproduce and find the cause.\n---\n\n# Debug locally\n\n1. Reproduce with the smallest command. For a test: `bin/rails test test/functional/issues_controller_test.rb -n test_show` with `PARALLEL_WORKERS=1` so that output and breakpoints stay in one process. For a page: `bin/harness dev`, then open http://localhost:3000 (login `admin`).\n2. Read the logs: `log/test.log` for tests, `log/development.log` for the server. The level is `debug` outside production, so SQL queries and parameters are there. `tail -f log/development.log` while you click.\n3. Stop in the code: add `debugger` (the `debug` gem is in the development and test groups), then run the single test or the server again. Remove it before you commit.\n4. Inspect data: `bin/rails console` (development database) or `bin/rails console -e test`. In tests, data comes from `test/fixtures/*.yml`; check which fixtures the test class loads (`fixtures :projects, :issues, ...`).\n5. Check the environment when tests skip or fail around SCM, LDAP or images: `Subversion test repository NOT FOUND` means `tmp/test` is empty or `svnadmin` is missing (run `bin/harness setup`); `Skipping LDAP tests` is expected without an LDAP server; Admin > Information (`/admin/info`) lists missing ImageMagick, Ghostscript, Pandoc or Mermaid.\n6. If the failure only happens in the full suite, look for order dependence: rerun with the seed that the output printed, `bin/rails test test/unit --seed 12345`.\n7. If boot fails, move any folder out of `plugins/` and retry; plugin Gemfiles and code load at boot.\n8. Once fixed, run `bin/harness check-fast` and write down anything surprising in `docs/harness/log.md`.\nEOF\ncat > .agents/skills/write-migration/SKILL.md <<'EOF'\n---\nname: write-migration\ndescription: Use when a Redmine change needs a new table, column, index or data migration in db/migrate.\n---\n\n# Write a migration\n\n1. Look at recent migrations for the style: `ls db/migrate | tail -5`. New files use a timestamp prefix and the current Rails version, for example `class AddAvatarIdToUsers < ActiveRecord::Migration[8.1]`.\n2. Generate the file: `bin/rails generate migration AddFooToIssues foo:string`, then edit it in `db/migrate/`.\n3. Keep it portable. Redmine runs on SQLite3, PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQL Server (see `docs/decisions/0002-multi-database-support-via-database-yml.md`). Use ActiveRecord methods (`add_column`, `add_index`, `change_column_default`) instead of raw SQL. When raw SQL cannot be avoided, branch on `Redmine::Database.postgresql?`, `.mysql?`, `.sqlite?`, `.sqlserver?` (`lib/redmine/database.rb`).\n4. Prefer a reversible `change` method; write `up` and `down` when Rails cannot reverse it. Do not use application models with their current code in data migrations: define a small model class inside the migration if you need one.\n5. Apply it to both databases: `bin/rails db:migrate` and `RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails db:migrate`. Check the rollback: `bin/rails db:rollback` then `bin/rails db:migrate`.\n6. Update the fixtures in `test/fixtures/` if the new column must have values in tests, and add a unit test for the new behavior.\n7. Run `bin/harness check`. To try PostgreSQL or MySQL locally, start one with `docker compose up -d postgres` and follow `docs/local-dev.md` (Other databases). CI runs all three databases in `.github/workflows/tests.yml`.\n8. `db/schema.rb` is generated and ignored by git: never commit it. Never edit or delete an existing migration.\nEOF\ncat > .agents/skills/frontend-stimulus/SKILL.md <<'EOF'\n---\nname: frontend-stimulus\ndescription: Use when a Redmine change touches JavaScript, CSS, icons or view templates (Stimulus controllers, importmap, stylesheets, SVG sprite).\n---\n\n# Change the frontend\n\n1. Find the existing piece. JavaScript lives in `app/javascript/controllers/*_controller.js` (Stimulus) and `app/javascript/wiki_formatting/`. Pins are in `config/importmap.rb`; `pin_all_from \"app/javascript/controllers\"` loads every controller, so a new file `foo_bar_controller.js` is available as `data-controller=\"foo-bar\"` without other changes. Older code still uses jQuery in `app/assets/javascripts`.\n2. There is no JavaScript build: write browser-ready ES modules, no TypeScript or JSX (see `docs/decisions/0003-importmap-stimulus-without-js-build.md`). Third-party modules are vendored in `vendor/javascript` and pinned with `bin/importmap pin <package>`.\n3. Wire the controller in the ERB view (`app/views/**`) or helper with `data-controller`, `data-action` and `data-<name>-target` attributes. Follow an existing controller, for example `clipboard_controller.js` and the views that use it (`grep -rn 'data-controller=\"clipboard\"' app/views app/helpers`).\n4. Styles go in `app/assets/stylesheets/application.css` (or the matching file such as `gantt.css`, `responsive.css`). Colors come from `open-color.css` variables. Check right-to-left and small screens (`responsive.css`).\n5. Icons: use the `sprite_icon('name', label)` helper (`app/helpers/icons_helper.rb`). Icon names map to Tabler icons in `config/icon_source.yml`; after adding one, regenerate `app/assets/images/icons.svg` with `bin/rails icons:generate` (needs network).\n6. Strings shown to users go in `config/locales/en.yml` and are passed to JavaScript through data attributes or the view, not hard-coded in JavaScript.\n7. Lint: `bin/harness lint` (RuboCop and Stylelint with `.stylelintrc`).\n8. Test the behavior in a system test in `test/system/` (Capybara and Selenium): `bin/rails test test/system/copy_to_clipboard_test.rb`, or all of them with `bin/harness test-system`. They need Google Chrome; set `GOOGLE_CHROME_OPTS_ARGS=headless` to hide the browser. Mermaid tests also need `bin/rails redmine:mermaid:install RAILS_ENV=test`.\n9. Finish with `bin/harness check-fast`.\nEOF\nls app/assets/javascripts | head -5; grep -rln 'data-controller=\"clipboard\"\\|controller: \"clipboard\"\\|clipboard' app/views app/helpers | head -3; grep -n \"pin\\b\\|pin \" bin/importmap | head -2; grep -n \"Redmine::Database.sqlite?\" -r app lib | head -1"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":687,"cache_read_input_tokens":126174,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":687},"output_tokens":29,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98c9b43a-1aa1-4043-94cd-7a9dd1b3395e","uuid":"5e7bd186-fe6c-4b6f-9fb4-5d0e5bac6a0a","timestamp":"2026-11-01T16:01:03.832Z","request_id":"req_011CgcVCuJGQQP2AzgWP3TGr","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TRpv1oqzLY6z9DRaH4RnsW":{"command":"cd /Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/repo; mkdir -p .agents/skills/{implement-change,debug-local,write-migration,frontend-stimulus}\ncat > .agents/skills/implement-change/SKILL.md <<'EOF'\n---\nname: implement-change\ndescription: Use when you take a task (bug fix, feature, refactor) in Redmine and need to go from the request to a verified change.\n---\n\n# Implement a change\n\n1. Make sure the environment is ready: `bin/harness setup` (safe to run again). It writes `config/database.yml` for SQLite3 if missing, installs gems and prepares the databases.\n2. Find the code. Start from the route in `config/routes.rb`, then the controller in `app/controllers`, the model in `app/models` and the library code in `lib/redmine`. `docs/product.md` maps each use case to its entry points; `docs/architecture.md` explains the components.\n3. Find the existing tests for that code: `test/unit/<model>_test.rb`, `test/functional/<controller>_test.rb`, `test/integration/` (API tests in `test/integration/api_test/`), `test/system/` (browser). Test data comes from `test/fixtures/*.yml`.\n4. Write or update a test first and run it alone: `bin/rails test test/unit/issue_test.rb -n test_create` (use `-n /pattern/` for several).\n5. Make the change. Keep the existing style: RuboCop config in `.rubocop.yml`, hash rockets are common in old code, user-visible strings go in `config/locales/en.yml`.\n6. If the change needs a schema change, follow the `write-migration` skill. If it touches JavaScript or CSS, follow the `frontend-stimulus` skill.\n7. Run the fast gate: `bin/harness check-fast` (RuboCop, Stylelint, unit and helper tests, about 40 s). Fix every offense and failure. `bundle exec rubocop -a <file>` fixes most style offenses.\n8. When the change touches controllers, views, routes or the API, run the full gate: `bin/harness check` (all tests except system tests, about 3 minutes). For UI behavior, also run the system test you changed: `bin/rails test test/system/issues_test.rb` (needs Google Chrome).\n9. Write the commit message in the upstream style: one imperative sentence ending with the redmine.org issue number, for example `Fix missing Gantt expanders for projects with only subprojects (#44694).` See `docs/agreements/working-agreements.md`.\n10. Add a dated entry to `docs/harness/log.md` for a significant change: what changed, what you learned, what is left.\nEOF\ncat > .agents/skills/debug-local/SKILL.md <<'EOF'\n---\nname: debug-local\ndescription: Use when a Redmine test fails, a page errors, or behavior is wrong on your machine and you need to reproduce and find the cause.\n---\n\n# Debug locally\n\n1. Reproduce with the smallest command. For a test: `bin/rails test test/functional/issues_controller_test.rb -n test_show` with `PARALLEL_WORKERS=1` so that output and breakpoints stay in one process. For a page: `bin/harness dev`, then open http://localhost:3000 (login `admin`).\n2. Read the logs: `log/test.log` for tests, `log/development.log` for the server. The level is `debug` outside production, so SQL queries and parameters are there. `tail -f log/development.log` while you click.\n3. Stop in the code: add `debugger` (the `debug` gem is in the development and test groups), then run the single test or the server again. Remove it before you commit.\n4. Inspect data: `bin/rails console` (development database) or `bin/rails console -e test`. In tests, data comes from `test/fixtures/*.yml`; check which fixtures the test class loads (`fixtures :projects, :issues, ...`).\n5. Check the environment when tests skip or fail around SCM, LDAP or images: `Subversion test repository NOT FOUND` means `tmp/test` is empty or `svnadmin` is missing (run `bin/harness setup`); `Skipping LDAP tests` is expected without an LDAP server; Admin > Information (`/admin/info`) lists missing ImageMagick, Ghostscript, Pandoc or Mermaid.\n6. If the failure only happens in the full suite, look for order dependence: rerun with the seed that the output printed, `bin/rails test test/unit --seed 12345`.\n7. If boot fails, move any folder out of `plugins/` and retry; plugin Gemfiles and code load at boot.\n8. Once fixed, run `bin/harness check-fast` and write down anything surprising in `docs/harness/log.md`.\nEOF\ncat > .agents/skills/write-migration/SKILL.md <<'EOF'\n---\nname: write-migration\ndescription: Use when a 